      "claim": "The minimum cross-sectional diameter of an adult human skull is significantly larger than the maximum diameter of the human birth canal.",
      "evidence": "The biparietal diameter of an adult skull is typically 14-15 cm, and even the narrowest transverse diameter is approx 11-12 cm. In contrast, the widest part of the maternal pelvic inlet (the transverse diameter) is typically 12-13 cm, and the anteroposterior diameter is 11-12 cm. While the narrowest skull dimension is close to the pelvic inlet, the skull is not a uniform cylinder; its shape and the specific dimensions of the pelvic canal (which narrows at the mid-pelvis and outlet) make passage impossible. More critically, the 'minimum diameter' of the skull (often cited near the foramen magnum or narrowest point) is still comparable to or larger than the effective passage of the pelvis, and the skull's overall bulk prevents alignment for passage.",
      "weight": 0.4,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "The adult human skull is a rigid structure that cannot deform to fit through a smaller opening.",
      "evidence": "Unlike a fetal skull, which has unfused fontanelles and overlapping sutures allowing for molding (compression), an adult skull is fully ossified and rigid, preventing any significant deformation without fracture.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "The birth canal is a rigid bony structure that cannot expand to accommodate an object significantly larger than its normal dimensions without catastrophic fracture.",
      "evidence": "The pelvic ring is a stable bony structure. While soft tissues can stretch, the bony boundaries of the pelvis do not expand to accommodate a rigid object larger than the inlet without shattering the pelvis, which is not a physiological 'passing through'.",
      "weight": 0.3,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Therefore, an adult human skull cannot physically pass through the birth canal intact.",
      "evidence": "Combining the size mismatch (Step 1), the lack of deformability of the skull (Step 2), and the rigidity of the pelvis (Step 3), the object is too large and rigid to traverse the canal without destroying either the skull or the pelvis.",
